# Independent review: inert v2 cutover inventory and privilege preflight

Final review complete: no unresolved blocker in the bounded inactive inventory/preflight. The independently rerun frozen SQL file passed **41/41 cases with zero skips** in 36.10 seconds. Scoped ESLint on the new module and test exited 0 with an empty diagnostic log. Root owns aggregate verification and durable repository/handoff recording.

Read the current continuation marker, AGENTS.md, v33 source audit, complete new inventory/evaluator, new source/route document, actual SQL suite and frozen P5 SQL declarations. Independently extracted 31 exact public function signatures: nine legacy enabling functions, four legacy terminal/cleanup functions, three legacy metadata readers, one policy administration function and fourteen v2 functions. Compared all 60 public/private manifest entries to their frozen source declarations: schema, function name, argument names/types, result type, definer flag and source file match exactly. All 13 source hashes in the new document match the unchanged SQL files. Source searches found no P5/v2 runtime route wiring.

The module is an inert exact inventory plus catalog-only SELECT and evaluator. It does not connect to a database, install SQL, change privileges or infer deployment readiness. Mandatory explicit trusted-owner configuration and an unconditional `deployable:false` result keep privilege assertions separate from unknown actual host/Auth, function body identity, route wiring and quiescence guarantees. The catalog query is intended only for a trusted owned disposable connection; its built-in calls are qualified and no application function is invoked by the query.

Early review coordinated these boundaries with the module and SQL authors: exact overload inventory rather than wildcard revokes; PUBLIC defaults plus effective inherited EXECUTE for the selected app principals; fixed `pg_catalog, pg_temp` search paths; exact definer/helper/schema ownership; dangerous app-role ownership or owner membership; no private-schema access or helper execution; and preservation of exact close/reconcile/revoke while legacy enabling paths deny. Legacy readers are explicitly retained only for trusted reconciliation and policy administration only for its separate trusted administrative path. These are candidate decisions, not participant permission.

The final candidate also rejects unknown EXECUTE grantees and service grant options. PUBLIC default ACLs are expanded, and effective role execution is inspected separately. Exact function shape includes scalar return cardinality, argument names, defaults, modes and variadic status. The conservative selected-role rule rejects every non-self membership, including harmless ones, rather than claiming to model a deployed role hierarchy. Incoming role memberships/host authenticators, unrelated privileged actors, table/RLS/default privileges, function body identity and other namespace wrappers are explicit scope limits. A function owner may still invoke revoked legacy functions internally; reviewed frozen bodies and host routing are therefore distinct requirements.

## Findings and corrections

1. Independent review found that deriving result shape only from `prorettype` would treat `RETURNS jsonb` and `RETURNS SETOF jsonb` alike. The new checker now includes `proretset` and rejects set-returning drift. Root review additionally identified the NOINHERIT/SET ROLE path that effective EXECUTE checks alone miss. These and the related exact named/default argument and execution-delegation checks were strengthened before their expanded negative-control run. They are pre-execution review corrections; no failing pre-fix runtime result is claimed.
2. After the expanded 41-case run passed, independent review found that its two SET ROLE demonstrations still had `session_user=postgres`: changing `current_user` with SET ROLE did not establish the intended source principal's switch authority. Root independently reached the same finding. The tests now first prove a target switch fails with SQLSTATE 42501 without membership, then use the exact source `SET LOCAL SESSION AUTHORIZATION`, assert both session/current identity, demonstrate the target switch despite false original effective EXECUTE, and require the source role's specific `unsafe-role` finding.
3. Those stronger controls exposed fixture identity-restoration failures. Two 35/41 diagnostic runs and a minimal PGlite probe remain preserved. Explicit known-fixture `SET SESSION AUTHORIZATION postgres` plus role reset and both identity assertions now restore each control. This is local engine fixture behavior, not a deployed Auth or native PostgreSQL acceptance claim. No checker/application/SQL behavior or assertion was weakened to obtain the final pass.
4. Initial collection/import and legacy raw ACK-field fixture mistakes are recorded precisely in `cutover-sql-notes.md`: an extra relative-parent segment was corrected, then the new raw legacy assertions were corrected from `status` to the unchanged `state` field. Initial logs remain separate from final evidence.

The session-user distinction was checked against PostgreSQL 18's primary documentation: [SET ROLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-role.html) and [SET SESSION AUTHORIZATION](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-session-authorization.html). The former uses the session user's switching permissions; changing the session authorization is the relevant fixture operation. The final local negative/positive controls provide the actual evidence here.

## Final independent validation

The candidate baseline fails unchanged, while fixture-local exact grants/revokes produce the expected catalog status without changing catalog or lifecycle/accounting snapshots during preflight. All public functions are actually denied to anon/authenticated; all nine legacy enabling functions are denied to the service role; all private helpers deny app roles. Actual SQL negative controls cover direct/default/inherited/delegated grants, NOINHERIT membership, signatures and shape, owners, search paths/configuration, schema access and role-management drift.

Under the candidate, the existing real ConversationService → trusted issuer → admission → marker chain remains usable with exact issuer output. All legacy enabling calls deny. Exact base/capacity close, session revoke and reconciliation remain callable after expiry/revocation without new Auth. Unknown finality preserves the occupied marked hold; separately fictional trusted whole-operation not-created evidence releases the slot exactly once while cumulative units and quota remain consumed. Close-before-register remains terminal and does not bind or reopen its cleanup. Prior immutable records and unrelated baseline rows remain intact.

Both engine instances close and clocks restore. Tests keep the existing two-worker and 15000-ms per-test limits. `cutover-independent-tests-pre-session-authorization.log` preserves the earlier 41/41 run that did not yet establish correct source-session switch authority; `cutover-independent-final-tests.log` is the final valid 41/41 run. `cutover-independent-final-lint.log` is empty with exit code 0.

