# v34 cutover inventory implementation notes

Work is limited to two new repository files: `scripts/conversation-eval/p5-sql/cutover-inventory.ts` and `docs/pilot/conversation/p5-v2-cutover-preflight-2026-11-06.md`. Root supplied and verified baseline main `aa5d5846cf40ef9a008c667fba10ecb415d0589f`, tree `305994c8d2e15f03d91d63bb8816b85bfa343d14`. No existing app source, SQL draft, migration, prior test, generated integration, setting or history was edited by this agent. The separate SQL agent owns the new acceptance test. Root owns marker/evidence changes and final aggregate checks.

## Source audit and fixed candidate

Read AGENTS.md, latest CONTINUE_HERE block, latest P5 lifecycle checkpoint, lifetime-change contract and v33 source audit. Extracted exact function declarations from the frozen non-fixture SQL drafts: 31 public functions plus 29 private helpers. Public categories are nine legacy enabling functions denied to service; four retained terminal close/reconcile/revoke paths; three retained legacy metadata readers explicitly limited to trusted reconciliation; one retained separately trusted policy administration path; and fourteen retained service-only v2 functions. All public RPCs are SECURITY DEFINER; helpers SECURITY INVOKER; all use the sole fixed catalog/temp search path. Each manifest entry includes exact type signature, argument names, result, role decision and source file.

The module is inert: no imports, client, environment, network, CLI, credential access, grant/revoke, migration or runtime registration. One SELECT-only catalog query supplies a snapshot. The evaluator requires an explicit trusted owner and always returns deployable:false. It verifies complete scoped inventory, cardinality/call shape, security mode, owner, configuration, PUBLIC/default ACL, effective application role execution, direct execute grantees, service grant option and schema privileges. Application roles cannot be superusers/CREATEROLE, members of owners or members of any other role. The latter is intentionally conservative rather than a deployed role-policy engine.

All public bfa_p5_ overloads and every function in the three private schemas are examined. PUBLIC/anon/authenticated execute deny everywhere; service execute only the selected public entries; no app helper or private-schema access; public schema USAGE retained/CREATE denied. The actor scope, incoming-role/host-authenticator gap, caller-supplied snapshot provenance, body authenticity, schema/table/RLS/default-privilege scope and unsupported hosted facts are explicit. The checker does not pretend privilege metadata authenticates implementation bytes or host routing.

